@* The shared parchment popover (design-direction.md: "popovers and hover
   cards are parchment artifacts on both pages... One popover style
   everywhere -- it renders the same ExplorerPopover -- the design mirrors
   the architecture"). Renders ANY IExplorable: title, a body built from the
   Batch R section-provider REGISTRY (Explore/PopoverSections.cs), the
   pre-existing exploration chips, and a breadcrumb Stack for drilling in
   (Push) and walking back out.

   Batch R requirement 3 ("the popover becomes a content-first section
   platform"): the body is no longer one node-specific IExplorable.BodyAsync
   fragment. LoadCurrent asks PopoverSectionRegistry.Providers which ones
   apply to the CURRENT node's own Kind (Verse/Passage/Place, today); each
   applicable one resolves independently (conditional presence -- null means
   no section), and every non-null result renders, IN REGISTRY ORDER, inside
   .popover-body. A node kind NO provider claims (Chapter/Book/Author/
   TimeAndPlace/Year) falls back to that node's own BodyAsync, UNCHANGED --
   this file's pre-Batch-R rendering path for those five kinds, byte-for-byte.
   This component implements IPopoverSectionContext itself (PushAsync below)
   -- a provider's own section can drill into a new node the exact same way
   a chip already could, without ExplorerPopover growing a bespoke callback
   per provider ("no popover surgery" for Batch F/P, whichever content they
   add).

   One node-specific behavior stays special-cased here (unrelated to the
   registry): any ShowMiniMap-targeted chip reveals a mini-map area IN PLACE
   (no stack push) -- CONTRACT's mini-map testid lives inside the still-open
   popover beside its own chips/breadcrumb, not as a separate node. Renders a
   real <MiniWorld> (Task 15); MiniWorld's own root carries the mini-map
   testid, so this file adds no extra wrapping element around it. (The old
   popover-chip-xrefs toggle -- VerseNode/PassageNode's own retired chip --
   and its ToggleXrefs/_xrefs machinery are GONE: cross-references render
   inline now, via the registry's own CrossRefsSection, "no extra button
   press" per requirement 3(b). Same for PlaceNode's own OnSelectEvent wiring
   -- PlaceEventsSection pushes TimeAndPlaceNode via PushAsync directly now,
   so PlaceNode's own BodyAsync/OnSelectEvent survive only as its defensive,
   never-called-in-practice IExplorable fallback -- see that class's own
   comment.)

   Backdrop is a sibling element, not a wrapper, so clicking the popover
   itself never needs stopPropagation to avoid closing through a parent
   listener -- a click on the popover simply never reaches the backdrop's
   own @onclick at all.

   Batch F2 requirement 6d ("the hover windows ought not be smack dab in
   the center of the screen, but on the side of the screen where the hover
   exploration originated"): PaneAnchor (set by Reader.razor/World.razor
   from their own split state -- "reader"/"atlas"/null) anchors the popover
   to the ORIGINATING PANE's currently-visible region instead of the full
   viewport, while a split is open. Measured ONCE, on this instance's first
   render (mirrors PlaceCard/CardPlacement's own proven "snapshot at open,
   never re-measure on scroll/resize" discipline -- see that file's own
   comment for why a continuous tracker isn't needed here either) via
   reader.js's getPaneRect (viewport-clamped, so it's correct even when the
   reader pane's own content is taller than one screen -- see that
   function's own comment). Full-page (non-split) behavior is byte-for-byte
   unchanged: PaneAnchor is null there, so .popover-pane-anchored is never
   applied and every rule falls back to its pre-existing, viewport-centered
   base. *@
@using System.Globalization
@inject AtlasClient Atlas
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@implements IPopoverSectionContext

<div class="popover-backdrop @(_paneRect is not null ? "popover-pane-anchored" : null)"
     style="@PaneRectStyle" data-testid="popover-backdrop" @onclick="RequestClose"></div>

<div class="popover @(_paneRect is not null ? "popover-pane-anchored" : null)"
     style="@PaneRectStyle" data-testid="popover" tabindex="-1" @ref="_panelRef" @onkeydown="OnKeyDown">
    <div class="popover-head">
        @if (_stack.Count > 1)
        {
            <button type="button" class="popover-breadcrumb-back" data-testid="popover-breadcrumb-back"
                    aria-label="Back" @onclick="Back">&lsaquo;</button>
        }
        <p class="popover-title" data-testid="popover-title" data-ref="@(IsRefKind(Current.Kind) ? "true" : "false")">@Current.Title</p>
        <button type="button" class="popover-close" data-testid="popover-close" aria-label="Close" @onclick="RequestClose">&times;</button>
    </div>

    @* Batch R requirement 3: keyed by the CURRENT node's own Title (changes
       on every push/pop -- every node kind's Title is unique to what it
       names, e.g. a vref, a place name, a composed label) so every section's
       own rendered subtree -- INCLUDING any child component a section opens
       (VerseTextSection's own expanded/fetched-chapter state, in
       particular) -- is torn down and rebuilt fresh on every navigation,
       never silently reused across two different nodes that happen to
       render a same-shaped section in the same registry slot. A "body:"
       prefix (NOT the bare Title) -- fix, a real bug caught live: a
       VerseNode's own Title and its ShowMiniMap chip's own SRef are
       routinely the EXACT SAME string (e.g. both "GEN.1.1"), and
       <MiniWorld @key="miniMapSref"> below is this element's own SIBLING,
       not a descendant -- Blazor's own key-uniqueness check is scoped to
       the sibling group being diffed regardless of element/component type,
       so two unrelated siblings sharing the bare "GEN.1.1" key crashed the
       renderer outright ("More than one sibling... has the same key
       value") the instant a mini-map was opened. *@
    <div class="popover-body" data-testid="popover-body" @key="@($"body:{Current.Title}")">
        @if (_usingSectionRegistry)
        {
            @foreach (var section in _sections)
            {
                <div class="popover-section" data-testid="popover-section-@section.Testid">
                    @section.Body
                </div>
            }
        }
        else
        {
            @_body
        }
    </div>

    @if (_explorations.Count > 0)
    {
        <div class="popover-chips">
            @foreach (var ex in _explorations)
            {
                <button type="button" class="popover-chip" data-testid="@ex.ChipTestId" @onclick="() => Activate(ex)">@ex.Label</button>
            }
        </div>
    }

    @if (_miniMapSref is { } miniMapSref)
    {
        <MiniWorld @key="miniMapSref" SRef="@miniMapSref" />
    }
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IExplorable Root { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnClose { get; set; }

    // Batch H (split-view study, "no nested-popup rule"): true when this
    // popover instance is currently sharing the screen with the OTHER pane
    // (Reader.razor passes its own _splitOpen; World.razor passes its own
    // SplitMode) -- the one signal Activate's NavigateWorld case below needs
    // to know "a second full atlas must never open here; target the pane
    // that's already showing instead." OnNavigateWorldInSplit only needs a
    // delegate when InSplit is true (Reader.razor supplies one; standalone
    // Reader/World never do, and InSplit stays false there, so the fallback
    // Nav.NavigateTo branch is exactly today's unchanged behavior).
    [Parameter] public bool InSplit { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<string> OnNavigateWorldInSplit { get; set; }

    // Batch F2 requirement 6d: "reader" | "atlas" | null (the host page's
    // own split state -- see this file's own header). Which CSS selector
    // PaneRectMeasured below measures is derived from this, never hardcoded
    // -- see PaneSelector.
    [Parameter] public string? PaneAnchor { get; set; }

    private readonly Stack<IExplorable> _stack = new();
    private IExplorable Current => _stack.Peek();

    private IReadOnlyList<Exploration> _explorations = Array.Empty<Exploration>();
    private RenderFragment _body = _ => { };
    // Batch R requirement 3: true when >=1 registered provider claims the
    // CURRENT node's own Kind -- Verse/Passage/Place today (see
    // PopoverSectionRegistry's own doc comment for exactly which). Decides
    // which of _sections/_body the markup above renders; a node kind no
    // provider claims (Chapter/Book/Author/TimeAndPlace/Year) leaves this
    // false and _body is exactly this file's own pre-Batch-R rendering.
    private bool _usingSectionRegistry;
    private IReadOnlyList<PopoverSection> _sections = Array.Empty<PopoverSection>();
    private string? _miniMapSref;
    private ElementReference _panelRef;
    private bool _focused;

    // Batch F2 requirement 6d: the originating pane's own currently-visible
    // rect, in px, measured ONCE (PaneMeasured guards this, same one-shot
    // shape _focused already uses just below) -- null whenever PaneAnchor
    // is null (full-page, unchanged behavior) OR the measurement hasn't
    // landed yet (the popover renders at its ordinary viewport-centered
    // default for that one instant, same graceful-degrade-to-default
    // discipline PlaceCard/CardPlacement already established).
    private PaneRectDto? _paneRect;
    private bool _paneMeasured;

    // Real, live-caught bug (fixed before this ever shipped, not guessed):
    // Reader.razor's own split-pane root always carries data-testid
    // "reader-root" (its OWN CSS class toggles between "reader-page" and
    // "reader-page split-pane-reader", but the TESTID attribute never
    // changes) -- a selector targeting `[data-testid="split-pane-reader"]`
    // matches NOTHING, ever. World.razor's own root is the opposite: BOTH
    // its class AND its testid switch to "split-pane-atlas" together while
    // SplitMode. reader-root only ever needs measuring while PaneAnchor is
    // "reader" (i.e. while _splitOpen is true on the host), so it's always
    // carrying the split-pane-reader CLASS at exactly the moment this
    // selector is used -- no need to select on that class specifically.
    private string PaneSelector => PaneAnchor switch
    {
        "reader" => "[data-testid=\"reader-root\"]",
        "atlas" => "[data-testid=\"split-pane-atlas\"]",
        _ => "",
    };

    // Centers the popover ON the pane's own rect (transform: translate(-50%,-50%)
    // already applies, same as the base rule) by treating the rect's own
    // midpoint as the anchor point, and bounds its max-width/max-height to
    // the pane's own size (minus the SAME margins the base, full-viewport
    // rule already subtracts) so it can never overflow into the other pane.
    private string? PaneRectStyle => _paneRect is { } r
        ? $"--pane-center-x:{(r.Left + r.Width / 2).ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)}px;" +
          $"--pane-center-y:{(r.Top + r.Height / 2).ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)}px;" +
          $"--pane-width:{r.Width.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)}px;" +
          $"--pane-height:{r.Height.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)}px;"
        : null;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        _stack.Push(Root);
        await LoadCurrent();
    }

    // Focuses the panel exactly once, on the FIRST render of this instance
    // (a fresh ExplorerPopover is created per "opening" -- see Reader.razor's
    // @key -- so this naturally re-runs for every new popover without ever
    // stealing focus back on an internal push/pop/xrefs-expand within an
    // already-open one).
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!_paneMeasured && PaneAnchor is not null)
        {
            _paneMeasured = true;
            try
            {
                var readerJs = await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", "./js/reader.js");
                _paneRect = await readerJs.InvokeAsync<PaneRectDto?>("getPaneRect", PaneSelector);
                if (_paneRect is not null)
                {
                    StateHasChanged();
                }
            }
            catch (Exception)
            {
                // Positioning is a nicety, not load-bearing -- same graceful-
                // degradation stance CardPlacement's own measurement takes: a
                // failed measurement just leaves the popover at its default,
                // full-viewport-centered placement.
            }
        }

        if (_focused)
        {
            return;
        }

        _focused = true;
        try
        {
            await _panelRef.FocusAsync();
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Non-fatal -- focus is a nicety (keyboard Escape-to-close, a
            // visible focus ring on open), never load-bearing for the
            // popover's own functionality.
        }
    }

    // Batch R requirement 3: builds this node's own popover body. Two
    // disjoint paths, decided by whether ANY registered provider claims the
    // current node's Kind at all (_usingSectionRegistry):
    //   - Verse/Passage/Place: every APPLICABLE provider's own ResolveAsync
    //     runs CONCURRENTLY (Task.WhenAll, same "independent fetches never
    //     serialize" rule Batch G1 already established for
    //     ExploreAsync/BodyAsync below), and every non-null result becomes
    //     one _sections entry, IN REGISTRY ORDER (List.FindAll/Select
    //     preserve source order; Task.WhenAll resolves all of them before
    //     this method returns, so the ORDER the results are READ back in --
    //     the original applicable list's own order -- is what actually
    //     renders, regardless of which fetch happened to finish first).
    //   - every other kind (Chapter/Book/Author/TimeAndPlace/Year): the
    //     exact pre-Batch-R path, byte-for-byte -- Current.BodyAsync.
    // ExploreAsync (the chips row) runs in EITHER case, alongside whichever
    // path is taken, same concurrency reasoning.
    private async Task LoadCurrent()
    {
        _miniMapSref = null;
        // Fix, a real bug caught live (diagnostic script against the actual
        // running app, not static review): Blazor's own event-dispatch
        // machinery renders once at THIS method's first genuine await (the
        // fetches below) regardless of how far LoadCurrent has gotten --
        // Current.Title already reflects the NEW node by then (_stack.Push
        // always happens synchronously, before LoadCurrent is ever called),
        // but _sections/_explorations/_body were, before this fix, still
        // whatever the PREVIOUS node left behind, so that intermediate
        // render showed the new title beside the WRONG node's content for
        // the full duration of the fetch -- reproduced live via a
        // cross-reference chain: HAB.1.7's own 6-item xref list stayed on
        // screen under a "JER.52.25" title (a verse with only 3) until the
        // real fetch resolved. Clearing here makes that intermediate frame
        // EMPTY instead of WRONG -- the same "loading, then filled" shape
        // this method's very first caller (OnInitializedAsync) already had,
        // now applied uniformly to every push/pop, not just the initial
        // open.
        _sections = Array.Empty<PopoverSection>();
        _explorations = Array.Empty<Exploration>();
        _body = _ => { };

        var applicable = PopoverSectionRegistry.Providers.Where(p => p.AppliesTo(Current)).ToList();
        _usingSectionRegistry = applicable.Count > 0;

        try
        {
            var explorationsTask = Current.ExploreAsync(Atlas);

            if (_usingSectionRegistry)
            {
                var sectionTasks = applicable.Select(p => p.ResolveAsync(Current, Atlas, this)).ToList();
                await Task.WhenAll(sectionTasks.Cast<Task>().Append(explorationsTask));
                _sections = sectionTasks.Select(t => t.Result).Where(s => s is not null).Select(s => s!).ToList();
                _explorations = explorationsTask.Result;
            }
            else
            {
                var bodyTask = Current.BodyAsync(Atlas);
                await Task.WhenAll(explorationsTask, bodyTask);
                _sections = Array.Empty<PopoverSection>();
                _explorations = explorationsTask.Result;
                _body = bodyTask.Result;
            }
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            _explorations = Array.Empty<Exploration>();
            _sections = Array.Empty<PopoverSection>();
            _usingSectionRegistry = false;
            _body = builder =>
            {
                builder.OpenElement(0, "p");
                builder.AddAttribute(1, "class", "popover-meta");
                builder.AddContent(2, "Couldn't load this — check your connection and try again.");
                builder.CloseElement();
            };
        }

        await SyncNarrativeFocusAsync();
    }

    // Batch N requirement 3 ("map focus sync -- the same graph, seen
    // twice"): a SECOND small, node-specific special case in this file,
    // alongside the pre-existing ShowMiniMap one this file's own header
    // comment already documents -- map-focus is a cross-cutting concern
    // (it targets the MAP, not this popover's own body), not a section, so
    // it does not belong in the registry the way PRIOR/FOLLOWING's own
    // CONTENT does (see Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs). Runs at the
    // end of EVERY LoadCurrent (the initial open, every push, every pop,
    // every chip Activate that re-resolves Current in place) -- HOUSE
    // PATTERN: synced continuously at the point Current actually changes,
    // never captured lazily at dispose time. `INarrativeAware` (a small,
    // focused interface -- Explore/EventNode.cs, retargeted Batch T; lived
    // in the now-deleted Explore/NarrativeEventNode.cs before) is how this
    // stays decoupled from concrete node types: EventNode is the ONLY
    // opt-in today (Batch T retired VerseNode's own implementation --
    // verse-level narrative-awareness moved entirely to EVENT membership,
    // see VerseNode's own doc comment); every other node kind (Place/
    // Chapter/Book/Author/TimeAndPlace/Year/Passage/Catechism/Verse) falls
    // into the `else` branch and clears focus, so navigating away from an
    // open EventNode to anything else never leaves stale emphasis on the
    // map. Reads the SAME memoized
    // NarrativePositionsAsync the Prior/Following section providers
    // already call for THIS SAME Current node -- when both run (the common
    // case), this is a cache hit, not a second network round trip.
    private async Task SyncNarrativeFocusAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            if (Current is INarrativeAware aware)
            {
                var positions = await aware.NarrativePositionsAsync(Atlas);
                var narrativeIds = positions.Select(p => p.NarrativeId).Distinct().ToList();
                var eventIds = positions.Select(p => p.EventId).Distinct().ToList();
                await MapInterop.SetNarrativeFocus(JS, narrativeIds, eventIds);
            }
            else
            {
                await MapInterop.SetNarrativeFocus(JS, Array.Empty<string>(), Array.Empty<string>());
            }
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Map focus is a nicety layered on top of an already-rendered
            // popover, never load-bearing for the popover's own
            // functionality -- same graceful-degradation stance
            // PaneRectMeasured/BlinkPlace already take for their own JS
            // interop calls.
        }
    }

    // IPopoverSectionContext: a provider's own section (VerseTextSection's
    // xref/date/event rows, etc.) drills into a new node THIS SAME way an
    // ExplorationTarget.Push chip already does. Explicit StateHasChanged()
    // -- load-bearing, not defensive, per the EXACT same reasoning
    // PlaceNode.OnSelectEvent's own (now-retired-as-primary) closure already
    // documented: a provider's own onclick is bound via
    // EventCallback.Factory.Create(ctx, ...) where ctx is passed as the
    // IPopoverSectionContext INTERFACE reference -- the underlying object IS
    // this component (so automatic re-render should, in principle, already
    // fire), but this call removes any doubt rather than leaning on that
    // subtlety a second time.
    public async Task PushAsync(IExplorable node)
    {
        _miniMapSref = null;
        _stack.Push(node);
        await LoadCurrent();
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    // Batch F2 requirement 6: see IPopoverSectionContext.OtherContextSectionCount's
    // own doc comment for why this reads `_sections` live (render time),
    // never a value snapshotted during the concurrent ResolveAsync phase.
    // "verse-text" (the subject) and "xrefs" (asking about its OWN
    // siblings, not itself) are excluded; every other resolved section
    // counts, generically -- no hardcoded catechism check.
    public int OtherContextSectionCount => _sections.Count(s => s.Testid is not ("verse-text" or "xrefs"));

    private async Task Activate(Exploration ex)
    {
        switch (ex.Target)
        {
            case ExplorationTarget.Push push:
                _miniMapSref = null;
                _stack.Push(push.Next);
                await LoadCurrent();
                break;

            case ExplorationTarget.ShowMiniMap map:
                _miniMapSref = _miniMapSref == map.SRef ? null : map.SRef;
                break;

            case ExplorationTarget.NavigateWorld world:
                await RequestClose();
                // Batch H: "the split IS the atlas" -- while a split is
                // showing, a chip that would otherwise open a SECOND full
                // /world instead hands its exact query (a scripture ref or a
                // from/to window, unescaped from ExplorationTarget's own
                // ready-to-append shape) to whichever pane is already
                // showing the atlas, in place. InSplit false (every
                // standalone page) keeps today's behavior byte-for-byte.
                if (InSplit && OnNavigateWorldInSplit.HasDelegate)
                {
                    await OnNavigateWorldInSplit.InvokeAsync(world.Query);
                }
                else
                {
                    Nav.NavigateTo($"/world?{world.Query}");
                }
                break;

            case ExplorationTarget.NavigateReader reader:
                await RequestClose();
                // Batch F2 requirement 6c: preserves an open split across
                // this navigation too (SPLIT-1's own "reader navigation
                // keeps the split open" promise, extended to this chip --
                // InSplit is true for EITHER pane's own popover while a
                // split is showing, so this is correct for a chip clicked
                // from the atlas pane's own popover as well). Query BEFORE
                // fragment, same reasoning as Reader.razor's own ChapterHref/
                // ApplyScriptureRef.
                var query = InSplit ? "?split=1" : "";
                var fragment = reader.Verse is int v ? $"#v{v}" : "";
                var url = $"/read/{reader.Book}/{reader.Chapter}{query}{fragment}";
                Nav.NavigateTo(url);
                break;
        }
    }

    private async Task Back()
    {
        if (_stack.Count <= 1)
        {
            return;
        }

        _stack.Pop();
        await LoadCurrent();
    }

    // Batch N requirement 3: "Popover closes / context ends -> arrows
    // return to normal." Cleared HERE, synchronously at the close ACTION
    // itself (the genuine mutation point), rather than in a DisposeAsync
    // this component doesn't have and, per HOUSE PATTERN, must not gain
    // for this purpose -- RequestClose is the one choke point every close
    // path (backdrop click, the close button, Escape) already funnels
    // through, same as it always was.
    private async Task RequestClose()
    {
        try
        {
            await MapInterop.SetNarrativeFocus(JS, Array.Empty<string>(), Array.Empty<string>());
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Same graceful-degradation stance SyncNarrativeFocusAsync above takes.
        }
        await OnClose.InvokeAsync();
    }

    private async Task OnKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Escape")
        {
            await RequestClose();
        }
    }

    // Verse/Passage/Chapter/Book titles are canonical refs (GEN.1.1,
    // GEN.1.1-5, GEN.1, GEN) -- design-direction.md's "canonical refs...
    // set them like instrument readouts" applies to popover-title too, via
    // this attribute (see .popover-title[data-ref] in app.css). Place/
    // Author/TimeAndPlace titles are proper names/composed labels, styled
    // as ordinary prose headings instead.
    private static bool IsRefKind(string kind) => kind is "Verse" or "Passage" or "Chapter" or "Book";

    // Batch F2 requirement 6d: reader.js's own getPaneRect return shape --
    // deserialized via Blazor's DEFAULT (camelCase) JS-interop JSON options,
    // not Wire.Options (this never crosses the real HTTP API either, same
    // reasoning as MapInterop.cs's own CardMeasurementDto).
    private sealed record PaneRectDto(double Left, double Top, double Width, double Height);
}
